ABSTRACT

A1 assumes guards are decidable in bounded time. A3 assumes plan preconditions are predicates over state. A4 assumes invariants are subsets of state. The Verifiable Agency Theorem assumes VERIFY is decidable in bounded time.

None of these assumptions are grounded without a predicate language. This document specifies that language — SPL (State Predicate Language) — and the VERIFY state that executes it.

SPL is deliberately small. It has no recursion, no unbounded quantification, no free variables, no side effects. It is a finite-domain, quantifier-bounded first-order language, decidability proven by structural induction over its grammar.

The choice of a small language is not a limitation. It is the decidability obligation made explicit. Any richer language would break the Verifiable Agency Theorem. Any weaker language would fail to express real safety properties.

Status: Specification. No reference implementation. No hardware validation. Machine verification pending.

