1. INTRODUCTION: WHY VERIFY COMES THIRD

1.1 The Dependency Chain

```
State (A4) + Plans (A3) + FSM (A1)
  ↓
Predicates (A5)
  ├── Guards (A1)          — decide transitions
  ├── Preconditions (A3)   — gate plans
  ├── Envelopes (A2)       — bound actions
  ├── Invariants (A4)      — preserve safety
  └── VERIFY (this spec)   — evaluate all of the above
```

Every other sub-spec assumes predicates work. A5 makes the assumption concrete.

1.2 The Two Obligations

A5 has two load-bearing obligations to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Obligation O1 (Decidability). Every predicate expressible in SPL is decidable in bounded time.

Obligation O2 (Determinism). Every SPL predicate returns the same verdict for the same state, always.

If O1 fails, the theorem's assumption "V and preconditions decidable in bounded time" fails, and Theorem 1's finiteness is not enough — Corollary 2 (Exhaustive Verifiability) fails.

If O2 fails, Corollary 5 (Determinism of Behavior) fails.

1.3 The Design Principle

The predicate language must be exactly as expressive as needed for safety-critical verification — and no more.

Every added expressive power is an added decidability cost. Every removed expressiveness is a safety property that cannot be stated. SPL is calibrated to the safety-critical norm: express invariants, preconditions, and envelope bounds, prove decidability, move on.

