2. THE STATE PREDICATE LANGUAGE (SPL)

2.1 Grammar

Definition 1 (SPL Grammar). SPL is defined by the following grammar:

```
predicate  ::= atom
             | "not" predicate
             | predicate "and" predicate
             | predicate "or" predicate
             | "forall" var "in" range ":" predicate
             | "exists" var "in" range ":" predicate

atom       ::= field op value
             | field "in" valueset
             | field op field
             | const_pred

range      ::= "[" integer ".." integer "]"
             | identifierset

value      ::= integer | constant | identifier
valueset   ::= "{" value ("," value)* "}"
field      ::= "s." path
path       ::= identifier ("." identifier)*

op         ::= "=" | "!=" | "<" | "<=" | ">" | ">="
const_pred ::= "true" | "false"
```

Closure rules:

· No free variables. Every variable is bound by a forall or exists.
· No function calls. Every atom is a comparison.
· No recursion. The grammar is finitely nested.
· No negation of quantifiers beyond the declared depth D_pred.

2.2 Atoms

Definition 2 (Atom). An atom is one of:

Form Meaning Example
field op value Compare field to constant s.world.brake_pressure <= 200
field in valueset Membership s.mode in {IDLE, PLAN, EXECUTE}
field op field Compare two fields s.meta.cycle < s.meta.task_budget
true / false Constants true

Rule: every field reference in an atom must be a valid path in the state schema (A4 §2.1). Invalid paths are rejected at ROM burn time.

2.3 Connectives

not, and, or — standard boolean semantics. Fully parenthesized at burn time; no precedence ambiguity.

2.4 Bounded Quantifiers

Definition 3 (Bounded Quantifier). A quantifier is bounded if its range is one of:

· A finite integer interval [a..b] with a ≤ b < ∞.
· A declared identifier set whose cardinality is finite and known.

Rule: unbounded quantification is not expressible in SPL. There is no syntax for forall x in Nat. The grammar forbids it.

This is the key design decision. Bounded quantifiers keep SPL decidable in bounded time. Unbounded quantifiers would make SPL undecidable (or decidable but with no cost bound), violating O1.

2.5 Nesting Depth

Definition 4 (Predicate Depth). The depth of a predicate is the maximum nesting of quantifiers. A standard constant D_pred bounds the depth.

Recommended: D_pred = 4.

Depths greater than D_pred are rejected at ROM burn time.

2.6 Purity and Totality

Rule P (Purity). SPL predicates have no side effects. Evaluation does not modify state.

Rule T (Totality). SPL predicates are total. Every predicate evaluates to a boolean value for every state in S. There is no "undefined" or "error" outcome.

These rules are enforced by the grammar: there are no assignment operators, no throw constructs, no partial functions.

2.7 Compile-Time SPL

Rule C (Compile-time compilation). SPL predicates are compiled at ROM burn time into a canonical evaluation form. The canonical form is:

· Fully parenthesized.
· Quantifiers expanded to declared bounds.
· Constant atoms folded.
· Redundant atoms eliminated.

The compiled form is what executes. No runtime parsing.

