3. DECIDABILITY

3.1 The Decidability Theorem for SPL

Theorem A5-D (Decidability). Every SPL predicate is decidable in bounded time.

Proof. By structural induction over the SPL grammar.

Base case — atoms. Each atom compares finite-domain values. The comparison costs O(1). Therefore atoms are decidable in bounded time.

Inductive case — connectives. If p and q are decidable in bounded time with cost c_p, c_q, then:

· not p costs c_p + 1.
· p and q costs c_p + c_q + 1.
· p or q costs c_p + c_q + 1.

All are bounded.

Inductive case — bounded quantifiers. If p is decidable in bounded time with cost c_p, and range has cardinality r < ∞ (Definition 3), then forall x in range: p costs r × c_p + r. This is finite because r is finite.

Closure. The grammar is finitely nested with depth bound D_pred. Therefore the total cost of any SPL predicate is bounded by a finite function of c_atom, r_max, and D_pred. □

Corollary A5-D1 (Cost Bound). The maximum cost of an SPL predicate is:

```
c_max = D_pred × r_max × c_atom
```

For typical values (D_pred = 4, r_max = 64, c_atom = 1), c_max = 256 cycles.

3.2 The Determinism Theorem for SPL

Theorem A5-Det (Determinism). Every SPL predicate returns the same verdict for the same state.

Proof. Every atom is a comparison of finite-domain values, which is deterministic. Every connective is a deterministic boolean operation. Every quantifier iterates over a finite, declared range in declared order. Therefore every SPL predicate is a deterministic function of state. □

3.3 The Totality Theorem for SPL

Theorem A5-T (Totality). Every SPL predicate is defined for every state in S.

Proof. Every atom compares values in finite domains. The comparison operators are defined for all values. Connectives are total boolean operations. Quantifiers iterate over declared ranges; each iteration evaluates a predicate in the same grammar; by induction, each is defined. □

